UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 4, Lesson 1: Looking at artworks: describing and responding', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Looking at an artwork has two steps. First describe what you can see: the subject, the colours, lines, shapes and textures. Then respond by saying what it makes you think or feel and giving a reason from the artwork. A good response uses the sentence pattern ''I think this feels calm because the colours are cool and the lines are smooth.'' Everyone can have a different opinion as long as they give a reason based on what is in the work, and are polite about other opinions.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nChoose a picture in a book or the one on screen. Write one sentence that only describes what you see and one that responds with a feeling and a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''I can see a tall green tree with a thick trunk and a red bird on a branch.'' and ''It makes me happy because the red stands out against the green.'' The first describes only, and the second gives a feeling with a reason from the artwork.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that saying ''I like it'' or ''it is nice'' is a full response. Opinions need a reason from the artwork, such as the colour, the lines or the subject, so other people can understand what you see.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPick three different artworks from books, a gallery website with an adult or your own home. For each write a title idea, one describing sentence, one responding sentence with a reason and one question you would ask the artist. Share one with a family member and listen politely to their view.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Looking at an artwork has two steps. First describe what you can see: the subject, the colours, lines, shapes and textures. Then respond by saying what it makes you think or feel and giving a reason from the artwork. A good response uses the sentence pattern ''I think this feels calm because the colours are cool and the lines are smooth.'' Everyone can have a different opinion as long as they give a reason based on what is in the work, and are polite about other opinions.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-VIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Pick three different artworks from books, a gallery website with an adult or your own home. For each write a title idea, one describing sentence, one responding sentence with a reason and one question you would ask the a Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using describe on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking that saying ''I like it'' or ''it is nice'' is a full response.. Correction: Strong answer: ''I can see a tall green tree with a thick trunk and a red bird on a branch.'' and ''It makes me happy. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that saying ''I like it'' or ''it is nice'' is a full response. Opinions need a reason from the artwork, such as the colour, the lines or the subject, so other people can understand what you see. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating describe on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with describe, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about describe with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for describe. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for describe. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["''I can see a tall green tree with a thick trunk and a red bird on a branch.'' and ''It makes me happy because the red","Thinking that saying ''I like it'' or ''it is nice'' is a full response.","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material.","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson."],"question":"Choose a picture in a book or the one on screen. Write one sentence that only describes what you see and one that responds with a feeling and a reason. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for describe; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking that saying ''I like it'' or ''it is nice'' is a full response.","Looking at an artwork has two steps.","''I can see a tall green tree with a thick trunk and a red bird on a branch.'' and ''It makes me happy because the red","Careful use of respond and explain why it matters with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with describe?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named respond and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Thinking that saying ''I like it'' or ''it is nice'' is a full response.","Name respond and explain why it matters only, without linking it to describe.","Apply describe to new material and name the respond and explain why it matters used."],"question":"Which response best applies describe with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on describe. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Thinking that saying ''I like it'' or ''it is nice'' is a full response.","''I can see a tall green tree with a thick trunk and a red bird on a branch.'' and ''It makes me happy because the red","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Which response best uses elements, techniques and audience effect for describe?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Thinking that saying ''I like it'' or ''it is nice'' is a full response.","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for describe.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about describe?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Example: In a painting of a boat at night, Mia says, ''I can see a small orange boat on a dark blue sea with a pale moon.","Thinking that saying ''I like it'' or ''it is nice'' is a full response.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Looking at an artwork has two steps. — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue.","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful."],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for describe?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking that saying ''I like it'' or ''it is nice'' is a full response.","Name respond and explain why it matters only, with no linked example.","Pick three different artworks from books, a gallery website with an adult or your own home.","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which independent response best applies describe to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about describe with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating describe on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using describe on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for describe. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with describe, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking that saying ''I like it'' or ''it is nice'' is a full response.. Correction: Strong answer: ''I can see a tall green tree with a thick trunk and a red bird on a branch.'' and ''It makes me happy. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w04-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise describe.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Visual Arts","success_criteria":["I can use describe with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify respond and explain why it matters.","I can practise an opinion with a reason independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a title needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Looking at an artwork has two steps.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Looking at an artwork has two steps."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about describe with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: In a painting of a boat at night, Mia says, ''I can see a small orange boat on a dark blue sea with a pale moon. The orange is bright, so my eyes go to the boat first. It makes me feel safe because the light looks warm in the dark.'' She described the colours, then responded with a feeling and a reason.\n\nTRY FIRST\nChoose a picture in a book or the one on screen. Write one sentence that only describes what you see and one that responds with a feeling and a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''I can see a tall green tree with a thick trunk and a red bird on a branch.'' and ''It makes me happy because the red stands out against the green.'' The first describes only, and the second gives a feeling with a reason from the artwork.","explain":"Looking at an artwork has two steps. First describe what you can see: the subject, the colours, lines, shapes and textures. Then respond by saying what it makes you think or feel and giving a reason from the artwork. A good response uses the sentence pattern ''I think this feels calm because the colours are cool and the lines are smooth.'' Everyone can have a different opinion as long as they give a reason based on what is in the work, and are polite about other opinions.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that saying ''I like it'' or ''it is nice'' is a full response. Opinions need a reason from the artwork, such as the colour, the lines or the subject, so other people can understand what you see. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Describing and responding to art.","caption":"See first, then say why.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Describe</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">tall tree, red bird</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Respond</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">happy, red stands out</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: In a painting of a boat at night, Mia says, ''I can see a small orange boat on a dark blue sea with a pale moon. Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: In a painting of a boat at night, Mia says, ''I can see a small orange boat on a dark blue sea with a pale moon. Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about describe with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: In a painting of a boat at night, Mia says, ''I can see a small orange boat on a dark blue sea with a pale moon. The orange is bright, so my eyes go to the boat first. It makes me feel safe because the light looks warm in the dark.'' She described the colours, then responded with a feeling and a reason. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"A weak and strong response.","caption":"Give a reason.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Weak</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">I like it</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Strong</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">calm because blue is cool</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about describe with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that saying ''I like it'' or ''it is nice'' is a full response. Opinions need a reason from the artwork, such as the colour, the lines or the subject, so other people can understand what you see. Here is a more helpful example: Example: In a painting of a boat at night, Mia says, ''I can see a small orange boat on a dark blue sea with a pale moon. The orange is bright, so my eyes go to the boat first. It makes me feel safe because the light looks warm in the dark.'' She described the colours, then responded with a feeling and a reason.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''I can see a tall green tree with a thick trunk and a red bird on a branch.'' and ''It makes me happy because the red stands out against the green.'' The first describes only, and the second gives a feeling with a reason from the artwork.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that saying ''I like it'' or ''it is nice'' is a full response. Opinions need a reason from the artwork, such as the colour, the lines or the subject, so other people can understand what you see. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about describe with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nLooking at an artwork has two steps. First describe what you can see: the subject, the colours, lines, shapes and textures. Then respond by saying what it makes you think or feel and giving a reason from the artwork. A good response uses the sentence pattern ''I think this feels calm because the colours are cool and the lines are smooth.'' Everyone can have a different opinion as long as they give a reason based on what is in the work, and are polite about other opinions. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 4, Lesson 1: Looking at artworks: describing and responding","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Drawing, line, shape and colour","week":4,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: In a painting of a boat at night, Mia says, ''I can see a small orange boat on a dark blue sea with a pale moon. The orange is bright, so my eyes go to the boat first. It makes me feel safe because the light looks warm in the dark.'' She described the colours, then responded with a feeling and a reason.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Describing and responding to art.","caption":"See first, then say why.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Describe</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">tall tree, red bird</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Respond</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">happy, red stands out</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A weak and strong response.","caption":"Give a reason.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Weak</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">I like it</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Strong</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">calm because blue is cool</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w04-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
